    # 51..65: Step-by-step reasoning / problem solving (15)
    step_data = [
        ("Lab testing for Starch: Iodine Test", "Describe the step-by-step laboratory procedure to test for the presence of starch in a slice of boiled potato.",
         "Student pours a whole bottle of iodine onto an unpeeled raw potato.",
         "1. Take a small mashed piece of boiled potato on a clean porcelain tile. 2. Add 2 to 3 drops of dilute iodine solution using a dropper. 3. Observe the colour change. 4. A change to blue-black confirms starch.",
         "Starch consists of amylose helical coils that trap iodine molecules, forming an intense blue-black complex. This is the definitive biochemical test for carbohydrates in Class 6 science.",
         "Step 1: Place a clean sample of food (mashed potato or bread) on a watch glass or white ceramic tile.\nStep 2: Prepare dilute iodine solution (add a few drops of iodine tincture to half a test tube of water until light yellowish-brown).\nStep 3: Using a clean dropper, add 2-3 drops of dilute iodine solution onto the food sample.\nStep 4: Watch closely for 30 seconds for any colour transformation.\nStep 5: If the brown reagent turns deep blue-black, starch is confirmed. If it stays yellowish-brown, starch is absent.",
         "Skipping dilution of iodine or failing to record exact blue-black colour transition.",
         "Blue ink appearing on a magic reveal coloring book when painted with clear water.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What result will you get if you repeat this iodine test on a piece of pure egg white?",
         "Ensure rigorous lab protocol documentation with exact reagent names and colour outcomes."),

        ("Lab testing for Protein: Biuret Test", "Outline the exact step-by-step method to test whether powdered pulses (dal) contain protein, specifying both chemical reagents required.",
         "Student uses only water and iodine to test for protein.",
         "1. Make a food suspension by adding 10 drops of water to powdered dal in a test tube. 2. Add 2 drops of Copper Sulphate solution. 3. Add 10 drops of Caustic Soda (NaOH) solution. 4. Shake well. 5. A violet colour confirms protein.",
         "The Biuret reaction detects peptide bonds: copper ions (Cu2+) in an alkaline medium complex with nitrogen atoms in peptide chains, producing a characteristic violet/purple colour.",
         "Step 1: Grind a small quantity of pulse seeds (chana/toor dal) into fine powder.\nStep 2: Transfer a pinch of the powder into a clean test tube and add 10 drops of water; shake well to make a slurry.\nStep 3: Using a dropper, add exactly 2 drops of 1% Copper Sulphate (CuSO4) solution.\nStep 4: Using another dropper, add 10 drops of 10% Caustic Soda (Sodium Hydroxide, NaOH) solution.\nStep 5: Shake the test tube thoroughly and allow it to stand undisturbed for 5 minutes.\nStep 6: Observe the color of the liquid: a distinct violet or purple coloration confirms the presence of protein.",
         "Confusing the order and quantities of copper sulphate (2 drops) and caustic soda (10 drops).",
         "Adding special dye to swimming pool water that only turns purple if someone adds chlorine.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What will happen if you forget to add the caustic soda and only add copper sulphate?",
         "Drill the precise 2-drop CuSO4 and 10-drop NaOH protocol for CBSE Class 6 practical exams."),

        ("Lab testing for Fats: Paper Patch Test", "Explain the step-by-step paper translucent patch test used to detect fat in a groundnut seed.",
         "Student thinks if paper gets wet from water, it confirms fat.",
         "1. Wrap a groundnut in a piece of paper and crush it gently without tearing the paper. 2. Remove the crushed nut and straighten the paper. 3. Hold paper against light: a translucent greasy patch that does not disappear when dried confirms fat.",
         "Fats are non-volatile hydrophobic lipids that soak into the cellulose fibres of paper, altering its refractive index so light passes through. Water creates a wet patch too, but water evaporates completely, leaving paper dry.",
         "Step 1: Take a clean, dry sheet of plain white writing paper.\nStep 2: Place one or two shelled groundnut seeds inside a fold of the paper.\nStep 3: Gently crush the seed with a blunt object (like a pencil or small wooden block), being careful not to tear the paper.\nStep 4: Remove the crushed seed pieces and flatten the sheet of paper.\nStep 5: Let the paper sit for 2 minutes to allow any moisture/water to evaporate completely.\nStep 6: Hold the paper up towards a light bulb or window: if a permanent oily, translucent patch remains that allows faint light through, fat is confirmed.",
         "Failing to wait for water moisture to dry, misidentifying a temporary water wet patch as fat.",
         "Spilling water on a paper bag dries invisible; spilling pakora oil leaves a permanent see-through stain.",
         "Physical activity", "How can you prove the greasy patch is fat and not water if you are unsure?",
         "Teach the definitive control test: thermal evaporation of water vs permanence of lipids."),

        ("Diagnosing a deficiency case", "A 9-year-old child presents with soft bent leg bones, swollen wrists, and slow growth. Diagnose the condition, identify the missing nutrient, and design a 3-step dietary correction plan.",
         "Student guesses polio or measles.",
         "Diagnosis: Rickets. Missing nutrient: Vitamin D and Calcium. Plan: 1. Ensure 20-30 minutes of daily morning outdoor sunlight. 2. Include daily dairy products (milk, paneer, curd). 3. Add eggs or fortified food.",
         "Rickets results from failure of osteoid bone matrix to mineralize due to insufficient active Vitamin D or calcium. Reversing it requires combined sunshine exposure and dietary calcium.",
         "Step 1: Analyze symptoms: bent bones (bow-legs) and soft wrists in a growing child are pathognomonic for Rickets.\nStep 2: Identify biochemical cause: lack of Vitamin D prevents intestinal calcium absorption, leaving bones soft under body weight.\nStep 3: Immediate lifestyle remedy: expose arms and legs to morning sunlight (UVB) for 20-30 minutes daily to stimulate endogenous synthesis.\nStep 4: Dietary adjustment: provide 500 mL of milk/curd daily to supply raw calcium and phosphorus building blocks.\nStep 5: Follow-up: monitor bone hardening and posture over 3 to 6 months.",
         "Confusing nutritional rickets with infectious neurological polio.",
         "A plaster cast needs water and powder to harden; bones need calcium and sunshine Vitamin D to harden.",
         "Diagram", "Draw an illustration of the leg bones of a healthy child versus a child with rickets.",
         "Teach systematic clinical deduction from physical symptoms to dietary prescriptions."),

        ("Calculating energy from a meal", "A snack contains 30 grams of carbohydrates, 10 grams of protein, and 5 grams of fat. Calculate the total energy in kilocalories (kcal) provided by this snack.",
         "Student adds the grams together (30 + 10 + 5 = 45) and says 45 kcal.",
         "Total energy is 205 kcal. (Carbohydrates: 30 × 4 = 120 kcal; Protein: 10 × 4 = 40 kcal; Fat: 5 × 9 = 45 kcal. Total = 120 + 40 + 45 = 205 kcal).",
         "Different macronutrients have different caloric densities per gram: carbohydrates and proteins yield 4 kcal/g, whereas fats yield 9 kcal/g.",
         "Step 1: Recall energy multipliers: 1 g Carb = 4 kcal, 1 g Protein = 4 kcal, 1 g Fat = 9 kcal.\nStep 2: Calculate carbohydrate energy: 30 g × 4 kcal/g = 120 kcal.\nStep 3: Calculate protein energy: 10 g × 4 kcal/g = 40 kcal.\nStep 4: Calculate fat energy: 5 g × 9 kcal/g = 45 kcal.\nStep 5: Sum all component energies: 120 + 40 + 45 = 205 kcal.\nStep 6: Conclude the snack provides exactly 205 kcal of metabolic fuel.",
         "Treating 1 gram of fat as providing the same energy as 1 gram of carbohydrate or protein.",
         "Exchanging dollar bills, euro notes, and gold coins requires multiplying each by its distinct exchange rate.",
         "None", "Which nutrient in that snack provided the greatest amount of energy per gram?",
         "Teach quantitative nutritional calculations using the 4-4-9 caloric conversion rule."),

        ("Comparing food groups across samples", "You have four unlabelled white powders: Powder A turns blue-black with iodine; Powder B turns violet with CuSO4 and NaOH; Powder C leaves a permanent translucent patch on paper; Powder D shows no reaction to all three tests. Identify each powder.",
         "Student guesses all four are different brands of flour.",
         "Powder A is Starch (Carbohydrate); Powder B is Protein (e.g. egg albumin or gelatin); Powder C is Fat/Oil (e.g. powdered butter or lipid); Powder D is an inorganic mineral/sugar (e.g. salt, chalk, or glucose).",
         "Each biochemical test reacts exclusively with a specific molecular bond: iodine with amylose coils, Biuret with peptide bonds, paper with lipid wetting. Non-starch sugars (glucose) do not react with iodine.",
         "Step 1: Analyze Powder A: positive blue-black with iodine -> contains complex starch.\nStep 2: Analyze Powder B: positive violet with Biuret reagents (CuSO4 + NaOH) -> contains protein.\nStep 3: Analyze Powder C: positive permanent translucent spot on paper -> contains lipid/fat.\nStep 4: Analyze Powder D: negative to all 3 macromolecule tests -> could be a mineral salt (NaCl), calcium carbonate, or simple sugar.\nStep 5: Conclude identification of biochemical categories.",
         "Assuming all carbohydrates turn blue-black with iodine (forgetting that simple glucose does not).",
         "Four keys tested on four locks: each key opens only one specific door.",
         "Virtual Lab", "If Powder D tastes sweet and dissolves in water, which simple carbohydrate could it be?",
         "Develop deductive reasoning from multiple biochemical test results."),

        ("Designing a balanced tiffin for a Class 6 student", "Design a balanced school lunchbox (tiffin) for a 11-year-old child using affordable everyday foods, and verify that all 5 nutrient groups plus roughage and water are included.",
         "Student packs two packets of potato chips and a bottle of fizzy cola.",
         "Tiffin: 2 Whole wheat vegetable parathas (Carbs + Roughage + Fat from cooking oil), a boiled egg or paneer bhurji (Protein + Calcium), a sliced guava or seasonal fruit (Vitamin C + Minerals), and a bottle of clean drinking water.",
         "A growing student needs steady energy for classroom focus, protein for growth, vitamins for disease defense, and hydration, without sugar spikes from processed junk.",
         "Step 1: Provide primary energy (Carbohydrates): whole wheat flour paratha.\nStep 2: Provide body-building material (Protein): paneer chunks or boiled egg or sprout salad.\nStep 3: Provide protective micronutrients (Vitamins & Minerals): vegetable stuffing (spinach/carrot) and a fresh guava.\nStep 4: Provide digestive transit aid (Roughage): unrefined wheat bran and guava fibre.\nStep 5: Provide cooking lipid (Fats): 1 teaspoon ghee/oil used to cook paratha.\nStep 6: Provide hydration (Water): 750 mL water bottle to drink throughout the school day.",
         "Packing high-sugar, high-salt ultra-processed snacks that provide zero micronutrients.",
         "Packing a survival backpack with food rations, tools, medicine, and water canteen before a hike.",
         "None", "Why is a whole guava better in a tiffin box than packaged tetra-pack guava juice?",
         "Encourage choosing whole natural fruits over sugary processed juices."),

        ("Investigating anaemia in a school health camp", "During a school health checkup, 30% of students have pale conjunctiva under eyelids, white fingernail beds, and complain of breathlessness during sports. Identify the condition, explain the biological mechanism, and outline dietary solutions.",
         "Student says the children have a lung disease from running too fast.",
         "Condition: Iron-deficiency anaemia. Mechanism: Lack of dietary iron reduces haemoglobin synthesis in red blood cells, impairing oxygen delivery to tissues. Solution: Eat iron-rich foods (spinach, jaggery, pulses, dates) alongside Vitamin C to enhance absorption.",
         "Haemoglobin contains iron at its core to bind oxygen in the lungs and carry it to working muscles and brain cells. Low iron starves muscles of oxygen during exertion, causing rapid fatigue.",
         "Step 1: Clinical signs: pale inner eyelids, pale tongue and nail beds, fatigue, shortness of breath on exertion -> classic signs of Anaemia.\nStep 2: Biological mechanism: deficiency of iron atoms impairs erythrocyte haemoglobin production, lowering blood oxygen-carrying capacity.\nStep 3: Dietary remedy: increase iron-rich plant foods (green leafy vegetables, jaggery/gur, lentils, chickpeas, sesame seeds).\nStep 4: Synergistic absorption: pair iron foods with Vitamin C (amla, lemon) because ascorbic acid converts ferric iron (Fe3+) to easily absorbable ferrous iron (Fe2+).\nStep 5: Public health intervention: distribute weekly Iron and Folic Acid (WIFS) tablets provided by the government.",
         "Treating anaemia as an isolated lung disorder rather than a blood haemoglobin deficit.",
         "A delivery fleet with only half its trucks running cannot deliver packages to the city on time.",
         "Diagram", "Look at a blood smear diagram comparing healthy blood (many dark red cells) with anaemic blood (few pale cells).",
         "Teach the biochemical link between iron, haemoglobin, and tissue respiration."),

        ("Demonstrating water loss during cooking", "A student heats 100 grams of fresh spinach leaves in a dry pan. After 10 minutes, the wilted leaves weigh only 12 grams. Account for the missing 88 grams step by step.",
         "Student thinks 88 grams of spinach was destroyed into thin air and vanished from the universe.",
         "The missing 88 grams was water present inside the plant cells; heating boiled and evaporated the liquid water as invisible steam into the air, leaving only 12 grams of dry plant matter.",
         "Fresh vegetables are composed primarily of water (often 85-95% by weight) that maintains cellular turgor. Heat ruptures plant cell vacuoles, vaporizing cellular water into the atmosphere.",
         "Step 1: Initial mass: 100.0 grams of fresh, crisp spinach leaves.\nStep 2: Heat application: pan temperature exceeds 100°C, the boiling point of water.\nStep 3: Phase change: intracellular water in plant cell vacuoles vaporizes into water vapour (steam).\nStep 4: Escape: steam dissipates into the classroom air.\nStep 5: Final mass: 12.0 grams of dry fibrous cellulose, minerals, and chlorophyll pigments remain.\nStep 6: Mass balance: 100 g - 12 g = 88 g of evaporated water.",
         "Believing matter is destroyed rather than undergoing a physical phase change (liquid water to vapour).",
         "Hanging a wet 500 g towel on a clothesline until it dries to 150 g: the missing weight is evaporated water.",
         "Physical activity", "If you held a cold metal plate over the cooking spinach, what would condense on its underside?",
         "Connect plant cellular water content to phase change and mass conservation."),

        ("Testing milk adulteration with starch", "Some unscrupulous vendors add starch to diluted milk to make it look thick. How can a student test for this adulteration in 3 simple steps at home?",
         "Student tries to smell the milk to detect starch.",
         "1. Take 5 mL of milk in a clean transparent glass. 2. Add 2 to 3 drops of dilute iodine solution. 3. Observe the colour: pure milk turns yellowish-brown (negative), while adulterated milk turns blue-black (positive for starch).",
         "Pure natural milk contains lactose sugar, fats, and proteins, but zero starch. Any blue-black colour reaction with iodine immediately proves illegal addition of cheap starch powder.",
         "Step 1: Pour a small sample (1-2 teaspoons) of the suspect milk into a clean spoon or clear bowl.\nStep 2: Add 2 drops of common iodine solution (or medical povidone-iodine tincture).\nStep 3: Observe colour change immediately.\nStep 4: Pure milk turns light yellowish/brown from the iodine reagent itself (no reaction).\nStep 5: If the milk turns blue or dark blue-black, starch adulteration is definitively proven.",
         "Assuming pure milk should turn blue-black with iodine.",
         "A smoke detector stays silent in clean air; it only rings if illegal smoke is present.",
         "Physical activity", "Why do dishonest milk vendors add starch or flour to watered-down milk?",
         "Teach citizen consumer science and food safety vigilance."),

        ("Comparing energy in raw vs cooked food", "A raw potato has crisp hard starch granules. When boiled, the potato becomes soft and easily mashed. How does cooking change the digestibility of starch step by step?",
         "Student thinks cooking creates brand new starch atoms that were not there before.",
         "Heat and water cause starch granules to swell, burst, and gelatinize, breaking rigid crystalline structures so human salivary and pancreatic amylase enzymes can easily digest them into absorbable glucose.",
         "Raw starch is tightly packed inside intact plant cell walls that resist human amylase. Cooking (gelatinization) unwinds these tight amylose chains, allowing human enzymes to hydrolyze them efficiently.",
         "Step 1: Raw potato starch: packed tightly in microscopic crystalline granules inside tough cellulose cell walls.\nStep 2: Heat + moisture: water penetrates the granules at 60-70°C; granules swell and burst.\nStep 3: Gelatinization: amylose and amylopectin chains unwind into an open, loose gel.\nStep 4: Enzymatic action: human amylase enzymes can now access and rapidly cleave bonds into maltose and glucose.\nStep 5: Conclude cooking vastly enhances carbohydrate bioavailability and digestibility.",
         "Believing cooking is solely for flavour and has zero chemical effect on nutrient bioavailability.",
         "A tightly knotted ball of string is hard to untangle; unrolling the string lets you cut it into pieces instantly.",
         "None", "Which enzyme present in human saliva begins the digestion of cooked starch right inside the mouth?",
         "Introduce salivary amylase (ptyalin) in the context of food digestion."),

        ("Calculating daily water intake sources", "A person drinks 1.5 litres of plain water daily. Yet medical guidelines say our body needs 2.5 litres daily. How does this person get the remaining 1.0 litre without drinking more plain water?",
         "Student thinks the person will shrivel up and die of dehydration.",
         "The remaining 1.0 litre comes from fluids in food: fresh fruits (watermelon/oranges are 90% water), cooked vegetables, dal, soups, milk, and tea, plus metabolic water produced during cellular respiration.",
         "We ingest significant water through solid and semi-solid food. Most fresh fruits and vegetables are over 80% water by weight, contributing heavily to total daily fluid requirements.",
         "Step 1: Identify fluid requirement: ~2.5 litres daily for an active individual.\nStep 2: Direct beverage intake: 1.5 litres from direct drinking water.\nStep 3: Liquid food intake: 0.5 litres from milk, tea, dal, and soup.\nStep 4: Solid food moisture: 0.3 litres from fresh fruits (apples, cucumbers) and cooked rice.\nStep 5: Metabolic water: 0.2 litres produced inside cells when glucose is oxidized (C6H12O6 + 6O2 -> 6CO2 + 6H2O).\nStep 6: Total intake: 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.3 + 0.2 = 2.5 litres balanced.",
         "Assuming drinking water from a glass is the sole source of bodily fluid intake.",
         "You get electricity from wall sockets, but your phone also gets power from its built-in battery.",
         "None", "Which common summer fruit contains over 92% water by weight?",
         "Prompt students to recognize watermelon and cucumber as hydrating foods."),

        ("Investigating vitamin loss during food storage", "Fresh spinach leaves kept in an open warm kitchen basket lose 50% of their Vitamin C within 48 hours, while frozen spinach retains 85%. Explain the steps driving this vitamin degradation.",
         "Student thinks microscopic bugs eat the Vitamin C molecules in the basket.",
         "In open warm air, plant cellular enzymes (ascorbate oxidase) and atmospheric oxygen oxidize ascorbic acid into inactive diketogulonic acid; freezing inactivates these enzymes and slows oxidation.",
         "Even after harvesting, plant tissues continue cellular respiration. Exposure to warm oxygen accelerates enzymatic oxidation of Vitamin C, whereas cold freezing arrests enzyme activity.",
         "1. Harvest: plant cells remain metabolically active but are cut off from nutrient supply.\n2. Atmospheric exposure: oxygen gas diffuses into leaf tissues.\n3. Enzymatic reaction: native ascorbate oxidase enzymes oxidize ascorbic acid to dehydroascorbic acid.\n4. Irreversible breakdown: warm temperatures hydrolyze the ring, permanently destroying vitamin activity.\n5. Cold stabilization: temperatures below 0°C freeze water and halt enzymatic molecular collisions.",
         "Underestimating the chemical vulnerability of harvested fresh produce to ambient oxidation.",
         "A cut apple turns brown when exposed to air; Vitamin C oxidizes and degrades invisibly in the same way.",
         "None", "How should fresh leafy vegetables be stored at home to preserve maximum vitamins for 2 to 3 days?",
         "Teach practical food storage in cool, covered, airtight containers."),

        ("Comparing protein content across meals", "Meal 1: 200 g boiled rice + 50 g fried potato. Meal 2: 100 g boiled rice + 100 g cooked toor dal + 1 boiled egg. Evaluate which meal provides better protein for a growing child, explaining the steps.",
         "Student picks Meal 1 because it has more total grams of food (250 g vs 201 g).",
         "Meal 2 is vastly superior: it provides approx. 18-20 g of high-quality complete protein (from dal and egg) along with essential amino acids, whereas Meal 1 provides only ~5 g of low-quality protein and excess starch.",
         "Growing children need amino acids to build muscle, bones, and blood. Rice and potatoes are predominantly carbohydrate energy foods with minimal protein, while dal and eggs are dense protein sources.",
         "Step 1: Analyze Meal 1 components: Rice (2.5% protein) + Potato (2% protein) -> total protein ≈ 5-6 grams.\nStep 2: Analyze Meal 2 components: Rice (2.5 g) + Cooked dal (7-8 g) + 1 Boiled egg (6.5 g) -> total protein ≈ 17-18 grams.\nStep 3: Evaluate amino acid quality: Egg provides complete reference protein; dal complements rice amino acids.\nStep 4: Conclude Meal 2 provides more than triple the protein and superior biological value for growth.",
         "Equating total weight or bulk of food with nutritional protein content.",
         "A 100 kg bag of dry leaves cannot support a roof, but a 20 kg steel beam can; protein quality matters more than plate bulk.",
         "None", "How many grams of protein does one medium boiled chicken egg provide?",
         "Teach quantitative meal evaluation for growth requirements."),

        ("Testing for minerals in food (Ash test)", "When a dried piece of bread or food sample is burned completely in a laboratory crucible until all black smoke and carbon disappear, a fine grey powder (ash) remains. What are the components of this ash?",
         "Student thinks the grey ash is just dirty charcoal carbon.",
         "The grey ash consists of incombustible inorganic minerals (calcium, iron, phosphorus, magnesium, potassium); all organic carbohydrates, fats, and proteins burned away as carbon dioxide and steam.",
         "Organic molecules contain carbon-hydrogen bonds that oxidize into gases (CO2 and H2O) when heated to high temperatures. Inorganic mineral ions cannot burn and remain behind as elemental ash.",
         "Step 1: Place dried food sample in a high-temperature porcelain crucible.\nStep 2: Heat strongly with a Bunsen burner (over 500-600°C).\nStep 3: Carbonaceous organic matter burns with flames: carbohydrates, fats, and proteins oxidize to CO2, H2O vapour, and nitrogen oxides.\nStep 4: Once black carbon disappears, cool the crucible.\nStep 5: The residual grey/white powder is total mineral ash (calcium oxide, iron oxides, phosphates, sulfates).\nStep 6: Conclude this proves the presence of indestructible mineral elements in biological food.",
         "Confusing organic carbonaceous matter with inorganic mineral elements.",
         "Burning a wooden chair burns the wood into smoke, but the iron nails remain behind in the fireplace.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Could you detect whether the original food contained calcium or iron by chemically analyzing this grey ash?",
         "Demonstrate the chemical distinction between combustible organic matter and incombustible inorganic minerals.")
    ]

