    # 66..80: Diagram, graph, or virtual-lab reasoning (15)
    diag_data = [
        ("Diagram: The 5 Food Groups Wheel", "Study the food wheel diagram divided into 5 sectors: Grains, Pulses/Meat, Fruits/Vegetables, Dairy, and Fats/Oils. Which sector occupies the largest slice, and which occupies the smallest slice?",
         "Student thinks Fats/Oils should occupy the largest slice because it gives the most calories.",
         "Grains (carbohydrates) occupy the largest slice for base energy, while Fats and Sugars occupy the smallest slice because they are needed only in small, concentrated amounts.",
         "A healthy food plate emphasizes bulk complex carbohydrates, plentiful protective vegetables and fruits, moderate body-building proteins and dairy, and sparse concentrated oils.",
         "1. Observe sector proportions on the food wheel.\n2. Grains & cereals: largest sector (approx. 35-40% of daily plate volume) for sustained energy.\n3. Vegetables & fruits: second largest sector (approx. 30-35%) for vitamins and roughage.\n4. Pulses, meat & dairy: moderate sector (approx. 20-25%) for structural proteins.\n5. Fats & refined sugars: tiny wedge at top (approx. 5%) to prevent obesity.",
         "Assuming the nutrient with the highest calories per gram should be eaten in the largest volume.",
         "A house foundation has lots of concrete, but you only need a thin coat of paint on the walls.",
         "Diagram", "Why is the fats and oils sector kept so small on a healthy food plate?",
         "Teach visual plate proportions for balanced dietary planning."),

        ("Diagram: Goitre thyroid swelling", "Look at the clinical diagram of a human neck showing a normal thyroid versus an enlarged thyroid (goitre). What shape does the normal thyroid have, and what mineral deficiency causes it to swell?",
         "Student thinks the thyroid is shaped like a circular ball.",
         "The normal thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the lower front neck; deficiency of dietary iodine causes it to swell into a visible enlargement called goitre.",
         "The thyroid gland uses iodine to produce thyroxine. When iodine levels in food and water drop, the pituitary gland overstimulates the thyroid, causing cells to multiply and swell in an attempt to trap any available iodine.",
         "1. Identify location: front of the trachea (windpipe) just below Adam's apple.\n2. Normal morphology: butterfly shape with two lateral lobes connected by an isthmus.\n3. Pathological state: massive enlargement of tissue forming a protruding lump in the neck.\n4. Etiology: lack of trace iodine mineral in diet.\n5. Conclude: Goitre.",
         "Confusing swollen throat lymph nodes with thyroid gland enlargement.",
         "A butterfly spreading its wings across the windpipe; when starved of iodine, the wings swell into balloons.",
         "Diagram", "Which table condiment is fortified with iodine in India to prevent this swelling?",
         "Use anatomical line drawings to illustrate endocrine deficiency pathology."),

        ("Diagram: Bow legs in Rickets", "Compare the two bone diagrams: Diagram A shows straight vertical leg bones; Diagram B shows outward curved, bow-shaped leg bones. Which diagram represents rickets, and what causes the bowing?",
         "Student thinks the bones in Diagram B are bent because the child was stepped on.",
         "Diagram B represents rickets; lack of Vitamin D and calcium leaves the growing bone shafts soft and unhardened, so the child's body weight causes them to bow outwards when standing or walking.",
         "Bones must be impregnated with hard calcium phosphate mineral crystals. Without Vitamin D, bones remain made of soft uncalcified collagen matrix that yields under mechanical gravitational loading.",
         "1. Observe Diagram A: femur, tibia, and fibula form a straight vertical structural column.\n2. Observe Diagram B: femur and tibia bend outwards in a curved bow shape (genu varum).\n3. Relate to physics: soft pliable pillars bend when supporting heavy upper body weight.\n4. Biochemical diagnosis: Vitamin D deficiency impairs calcium absorption, leading to uncalcified bone.",
         "Believing bone deformities in rickets are caused by external blunt trauma.",
         "Green young tree branches bend when you hang a heavy bucket on them; dry hardened wood stays straight.",
         "Diagram", "What happens to the width of the wrists in a child suffering from rickets?",
         "Teach structural biomechanics and mineralisation failure in rickets."),

        ("Graph: Vitamin C content across common fruits", "A bar graph shows Vitamin C (mg per 100 g): Amla = 600, Guava = 220, Lemon = 50, Orange = 45, Apple = 5. According to this data, how many apples would you need to eat to match the Vitamin C in a single 100 g portion of amla?",
         "Student guesses 2 apples.",
         "You would need to eat 120 apples (600 ÷ 5 = 120) to match the Vitamin C content of 100 grams of fresh amla.",
         "Bar graphs clearly demonstrate the vast quantitative superiority of indigenous fruits like amla and guava over popular foreign fruits like apples for specific micronutrients.",
         "1. Read bar heights: Amla = 600 mg/100 g; Apple = 5 mg/100 g.\n2. Calculate ratio: 600 mg ÷ 5 mg/apple-portion = 120.\n3. Interpret: while apples provide dietary fibre and carbohydrates, their Vitamin C content is negligible compared to amla.\n4. Conclude indigenous amla and guava are exceptional, cost-effective Vitamin C sources.",
         "Assuming the famous proverb 'an apple a day keeps the doctor away' means apples have the highest vitamins.",
         "Comparing a single 100-rupee note with one hundred 1-rupee coins.",
         "Graph", "Which fruit in that graph ranks second highest in Vitamin C content?",
         "Build quantitative graphical data interpretation skills in Class 6 science."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Simulating food tests on an egg", "In a virtual chemistry lab, an egg is separated into yolk and clear white. Test 1: Iodine added to white -> no color change. Test 2: CuSO4 + NaOH added to white -> deep violet. Test 3: Yolk rubbed on paper -> permanent translucent patch. What nutrients are present in egg white and egg yolk?",
         "Student thinks the egg white has starch because it is white.",
         "Egg white contains protein (Biuret positive) and zero starch (iodine negative); egg yolk contains fat/lipids (paper translucent patch positive).",
         "The egg white (albumen) is pure aqueous protein designed to cushion the embryo and provide water and amino acids. The yolk contains concentrated lipids and vitamins to nourish the developing chick.",
         "1. Test 1 (White + Iodine): stays yellow/brown -> starch is absent.\n2. Test 2 (White + CuSO4 + NaOH): turns deep violet -> high concentration of protein confirmed.\n3. Test 3 (Yolk + Paper): permanent translucent greasy patch -> high fat/lipid content confirmed.\n4. Conclude: egg white is primarily protein, while egg yolk is rich in lipids.",
         "Confusing visual whiteness with starchy carbohydrate content.",
         "A coconut has clear liquid water inside and thick fatty white flesh; an egg separates protein white from lipid yolk.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What would happen if you performed Test 2 (CuSO4 + NaOH) on the egg yolk?",
         "Guide students through systematic multi-reagent biological testing on complex foods."),

        ("Diagram: Cross-section of a grain of wheat", "Examine the labelled diagram of a wheat grain: 1. Outer Bran, 2. Endosperm, 3. Germ. Which part is milled into refined maida, and what valuable nutrients are lost when bran and germ are discarded?",
         "Student thinks maida is made from the nutritious germ.",
         "Maida is made exclusively from the starchy endosperm; discarding the bran and germ removes dietary fibre, B-vitamins, iron, and healthy oils.",
         "Whole wheat flour (atta) contains all three parts ground together. Industrial milling sifts out the fibrous bran and nutrient-dense germ to produce white flour (maida), stripping over 70% of its micronutrients.",
         "1. Outer Bran layer: rich in insoluble cellulose fibre, B-complex vitamins, and trace minerals.\n2. Tiny Germ (embryo): rich in Vitamin E, B-vitamins, unsaturated lipids, and protein.\n3. Central Endosperm: stores starch granules and gluten proteins.\n4. Maida manufacturing: removes bran and germ, retaining only endosperm starch.\n5. Conclude whole wheat flour is vastly superior in micronutrient and roughage content.",
         "Believing refined white flour is purer and therefore more nutritious than whole grain atta.",
         "Peeling the bark and leaves off a tree leaves only firewood; refining wheat leaves only pure starch.",
         "Diagram", "Why does whole wheat atta spoil faster than refined maida when stored in humid weather?",
         "Relate germ oil oxidation (rancidity) to shelf-life versus nutritional value."),

        ("Graph: Effect of temperature on Vitamin C retention", "A line graph shows Vitamin C percentage in vegetable soup over cooking time at 100°C: 0 min = 100%, 5 min = 80%, 15 min = 45%, 30 min = 15%, 60 min = 2%. What percentage of Vitamin C is destroyed after 30 minutes of boiling?",
         "Student reads the remaining percentage (15%) as the destroyed percentage.",
         "85% of the Vitamin C is destroyed after 30 minutes of boiling (100% - 15% remaining = 85% destroyed).",
         "Thermal degradation of ascorbic acid follows a steep exponential decay. Prolonged pressure cooking or slow simmering destroys virtually all Vitamin C in home cooking.",
         "1. Locate 30 minutes on horizontal time axis.\n2. Move vertically to find curve: intersects at 15% retention.\n3. Calculate loss: Initial (100%) - Remaining (15%) = 85% destroyed.\n4. Culinary takeaway: quick steaming or eating raw salads preserves vitamins far better than extended boiling.",
         "Misreading remaining percentage on the Y-axis as destroyed percentage.",
         "If you have 15 rupees left from a 100-rupee note, you have spent 85 rupees.",
         "Graph", "At what cooking time has half (50%) of the Vitamin C been destroyed?",
         "Strengthen graph reading accuracy and subtractive percentage reasoning."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Testing unknown food samples for nutrients", "In a virtual lab, a student tests three unknown food extracts (X, Y, Z): X turns blue-black with iodine; Y turns violet with CuSO4 + NaOH; Z leaves a greasy mark on paper and turns violet with CuSO4 + NaOH. Match X, Y, Z to: Groundnut, Boiled Rice, and Boiled Egg White.",
         "Student matches X to egg white because both are white.",
         "X is Boiled Rice (starch); Y is Boiled Egg White (pure protein); Z is Groundnut (contains both fat and protein).",
         "Many natural foods are composite foods containing multiple major nutrients. Groundnut is an oilseed rich in both lipids and proteins, producing positive results in both tests.",
         "1. Food X: positive for starch only -> matches Boiled Rice (high starch, negligible fat/protein).\n2. Food Y: positive for protein only, zero fat -> matches Boiled Egg White.\n3. Food Z: positive for both fat (greasy patch) and protein (Biuret violet) -> matches Groundnut (approx. 45% oil and 25% protein).\n4. Conclude individual foods often contain combinations of nutrients.",
         "Assuming every food item can contain only one single nutrient type.",
         "A tool can be just a screwdriver, or it can be a Swiss Army knife that has both a blade and a screwdriver.",
         "Virtual Lab", "If you tested cow's milk in this lab, which two tests would give positive results?",
         "Promote understanding of multi-nutrient composition in whole natural foods."),

        ("Diagram: Bleeding gums in Scurvy", "Examine a dental clinical illustration showing inflamed, swollen, purplish gums bleeding around the teeth necks. What nutrient deficiency causes this condition, and what structural protein has failed to form?",
         "Student thinks the teeth are bleeding because the person brushed too hard.",
         "The condition is Scurvy, caused by Vitamin C deficiency; the structural protein that failed to synthesize properly is collagen, which holds capillaries and gum tissues together.",
         "Ascorbic acid is the essential enzyme cofactor required to hydroxylate proline and lysine during collagen triple-helix assembly. Without collagen, blood capillary walls rupture under normal blood pressure.",
         "1. Observe visual pathology: spongy, swollen, ulcerated gingival gums bleeding at tooth margins.\n2. Biochemical etiology: lack of dietary Vitamin C (ascorbic acid).\n3. Structural failure: collagen synthesis ceases; connective tissue anchoring teeth and sealing blood capillaries breaks down.\n4. Treatment: administration of citrus fruits, amla, or ascorbic acid reverses bleeding within days.\n5. Disease: Scurvy.",
         "Dismissing bleeding gums as superficial dental abrasion rather than systemic collagen failure.",
         "Mortar holding bricks together crumbling away, causing the wall to leak water.",
         "Diagram", "What happens to healed surgical scars or old wounds in a patient suffering from severe scurvy?",
         "Highlight the profound systemic role of collagen in wound maintenance."),

        ("Graph: Daily calcium requirement across age groups", "A bar graph shows recommended daily calcium: Toddlers = 500 mg, Children (Class 6, age 11) = 1,000 mg, Adults = 800 mg, Pregnant women = 1,200 mg. Why do Class 6 schoolchildren need more calcium daily than fully grown adults?",
         "Student thinks adults are bigger so they must always need more calcium.",
         "Class 6 children are undergoing an adolescent growth spurt where their skeleton is rapidly growing in length and bone density, requiring massive calcium deposition into new bone tissue.",
         "Adults need calcium only for skeletal maintenance, muscle contractions, and blood clotting. Growing children must deposit new bone mineral every day to build their adult skeleton.",
         "1. Read graph: Class 6 child = 1,000 mg/day; Adult = 800 mg/day.\n2. Biological reason: adolescent peak bone mass accumulation.\n3. Long bones (femur, tibia) are actively elongating at epiphyseal growth plates.\n4. Insufficient calcium during this stage results in reduced adult height and weak bones.",
         "Assuming nutrient requirements are strictly proportional to adult body weight.",
         "Building a new skyscraper requires truckloads of concrete daily; maintaining an existing building takes just a few buckets for repair.",
         "Graph", "Which food group on the daily plate provides the most concentrated dietary calcium?",
         "Connect growth spurts and skeletal physiology to age-specific nutrient guidelines."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Effect of saliva on starch", "In a virtual lab, Test Tube A has starch solution + water. Test Tube B has starch solution + human saliva. Both are kept at 37°C for 20 minutes. Iodine is added to both: Tube A turns blue-black; Tube B stays light yellow-brown. What happened in Tube B?",
         "Student thinks the saliva bleached the blue color away like laundry detergent.",
         "Salivary amylase (an enzyme in human saliva) broke down the complex starch into simple maltose sugars, so there was no starch left to react with iodine.",
         "Chemical digestion begins in the mouth. Amylase cleaves the alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch coils, breaking them into smaller disaccharides that cannot form the blue-black iodine complex.",
         "1. Tube A (Control): starch remains intact -> reacts with iodine -> blue-black colour.\n2. Tube B (Experimental): saliva contains the digestive enzyme ptyalin (salivary amylase).\n3. Enzymatic hydrolysis: at body temperature (37°C), amylase breaks long starch chains into maltose.\n4. Post-reaction: maltose does not form a complex with iodine.\n5. Result: iodine stays its native yellow-brown, proving complete starch digestion.",
         "Believing digestion only happens inside the stomach and intestines.",
         "A pair of scissors cutting a long paper chain into tiny confetti pieces that can no longer hold a ribbon.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What sweet-tasting sugar is produced when starch is digested by salivary amylase?",
         "Demonstrate enzymatic chemical digestion using controlled comparative test tubes."),

        ("Diagram: The Digestive Tract - Nutrient absorption sites", "Look at the diagram of the human digestive system. In which organ does the vast majority of digested nutrients (glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins) get absorbed into the bloodstream?",
         "Student points to the stomach because that is where they feel full.",
         "The small intestine (specifically the jejunum and ileum), whose inner lining is covered with millions of tiny finger-like projections called villi that absorb nutrients into the blood.",
         "While the stomach stores and churns food with acid, over 90% of all nutrient absorption occurs across the vast surface area of the small intestine villi.",
         "1. Stomach: secretes acid and pepsin to break down proteins; minimal absorption (only water, alcohol, certain drugs).\n2. Small intestine: long coiled tube (approx. 6 metres long in adults).\n3. Villi & Microvilli: foldings increase absorption surface area to the size of a tennis court.\n4. Blood capillaries and lacteals: transport absorbed glucose, amino acids, and fats throughout the body.\n5. Conclude the small intestine is the master organ of nutrient absorption.",
         "Believing the stomach is the primary site of nutrient absorption into the blood.",
         "The stomach is the blender that purees the soup; the small intestine is the sponge that soaks up every drop of broth.",
         "Diagram", "What are the millions of microscopic finger-like projections lining the small intestine called?",
         "Teach digestive tract anatomy and structure-function specialization."),

        ("Graph: Protein quality (Biological Value) of foods", "A bar graph shows Biological Value of proteins: Whole Egg = 100, Cow's Milk = 90, Fish = 83, Soya bean = 74, Whole Wheat = 64. Why does whole egg have a Biological Value score of 100?",
         "Student thinks eggs scored 100 because chickens take good care of them.",
         "Whole egg protein contains all 9 essential amino acids in the exact proportions required by the human body for tissue growth and repair, with zero limiting amino acids.",
         "Biological Value (BV) measures how efficiently the body absorbs and incorporates dietary protein into human tissue. Egg protein is the reference standard against which all other dietary proteins are evaluated.",
         "1. Define Biological Value: percentage of absorbed protein nitrogen retained by the body.\n2. Essential amino acids: humans cannot synthesize 9 specific amino acids; all 9 must be present in correct ratios.\n3. Egg profile: perfect match for human tissue protein synthesis -> minimal nitrogen wasted in urine.\n4. Plant proteins: slightly lower BV due to one limiting amino acid (e.g. lysine in wheat, methionine in pulses).\n5. Conclude egg protein represents the reference biological standard.",
         "Assuming protein quality depends solely on the total weight of protein rather than amino acid completeness.",
         "A printer that has all four ink cartridges prints in full color; an egg provides all essential ink cartridges.",
         "Graph", "How can vegetarians combine plant foods to achieve a combined protein score close to 100?",
         "Reinforce the concept of mutual amino acid supplementation (cereal + legume pairing)."),

        ("Virtual Lab: Testing oil vs water emulsion in food", "In a virtual lab, Test Tube 1 has water + oil (shakes and separates into two layers). Test Tube 2 has water + oil + drops of soap/bile. After shaking, Test Tube 2 stays milky and uniform without separating. What biological process does this demonstrate?",
         "Student thinks the soap destroyed the oil atoms.",
         "This demonstrates emulsification: breaking large fat droplets into millions of tiny microscopic droplets suspended in water, which is precisely how bile salts in our digestive system prepare dietary fats for enzyme digestion.",
         "Dietary fats do not dissolve in watery digestive juices. Liver bile acts like a biological detergent, emulsifying fats so lipase enzymes have a massive surface area to digest them.",
         "1. Physical principle: lipids are hydrophobic (water-repelling) and coalesce into large floating globules.\n2. Limitation: lipase enzymes can only act on the surface of fat globules.\n3. Emulsification: amphipathic molecules (soaps or bile salts) coat tiny fat droplets, preventing them from fusing together.\n4. Result: a stable milky emulsion that dramatically multiplies surface area for enzymatic attack.\n5. Conclude: models mechanical bile action in the human duodenum.",
         "Assuming fats are dissolved chemically like sugar in water rather than emulsified mechanically.",
         "Washing greasy hands with plain water fails; washing with soap emulsifies the grease into water droplets.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which organ in the human body manufactures bile to emulsify dietary fats?",
         "Connect physics-chemistry demonstrations of emulsions to digestive physiology (liver and bile)."),

        ("Diagram: Xerophthalmia eye pathology", "Look at the medical diagram of an eye suffering from advanced Vitamin A deficiency: the clear cornea has become dry, cloudy, opaque, and ulcerated. What is this severe condition called, and can it be prevented?",
         "Student thinks cloudy eyes are always caused by getting dust in the eye.",
         "This condition is Xerophthalmia (keratomalacia), caused by severe, prolonged Vitamin A deficiency; it causes permanent irreversible blindness, but is completely preventable by eating Vitamin A-rich foods.",
         "Vitamin A maintains the moist epithelial lining of the conjunctiva and cornea. Without it, tears dry up, the cornea keratinizes into hard opaque tissue like fingernails, softens, and ulcerates permanently.",
         "1. Early stage: night blindness (poor vision in dim light, completely reversible).\n2. Intermediate stage: Bitot's spots (foamy triangular patches on conjunctiva) and dry eyes (xerosis).\n3. Severe stage: Keratomalacia (corneal ulceration, clouding, liquefaction, and total irreversible blindness).\n4. Prevention: biannual mega-dose Vitamin A capsules and diets rich in dark green leafy vegetables and orange fruits.\n5. Conclude nutrition directly preserves sensory organ integrity.",
         "Believing blindness is solely genetic or accidental rather than caused by dietary malnutrition.",
         "A car windshield that runs out of wiper fluid and gets scratched until it turns frosted like a bathroom window.",
         "Diagram", "What are the foamy triangular white patches on the white of the eye in early Vitamin A deficiency called?",
         "Introduce Bitot's spots as an early warning sign of Vitamin A deficiency.")
    ]

