         "Vitamin C is water-soluble, so excess amounts cannot be stored and are excreted daily in urine; Vitamin A is fat-soluble and is stored safely in the liver for months.",
         "The human body has no storage depot for water-soluble vitamins (B and C). Daily dietary replacement is mandatory to prevent gradual tissue depletion.",
         "1. Water-soluble vitamins: Vitamin C and B-complex dissolve in blood; kidneys filter excess into urine daily.\n2. Fat-soluble vitamins: Vitamins A, D, E, K dissolve in lipids; liver stores up to a 6-to-12-month reserve.\n3. Nutritional consequence: Vitamin C must be ingested frequently; fat-soluble vitamins can be ingested periodically.",
